  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA weak shortwave trough and surface cold front will cross the\nregion this evening/tonight. Enough convergence, moisture and\ninstability was observed along this front near the Lake Erie \nshoreline for scattered showers to develop. There have even been\na few lightning strikes/thunderstorms with this activity. While\nthe thunder should be confined to areas closer to the lake, \nscattered showers could maintain long enough to track across \nareas near and north of I-80 this evening as the front crosses. \nElsewhere, dry air (as seen in the ongoing 00Z PIT upper air \nflight) should preclude shower development. \n\nOtherwise, dry weather then continues through Friday and \nSaturday as surface high pressure remains in control. \nTemperatures remain a a bit above climatology through Friday. \nSoutherly flow and rising heights behind the departing high may \npush daytime highs some 10 degrees above climatology on \nSaturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCurrent NHC guidance still has Isaias landfalling on the Gulf\nCoast Friday night, and then its remnant low reaching the Lower\nOhio Valley later Sunday night or early Monday morning. Model\nguidance starts to diverge thereafter on the exact track, but\nmost have the low turning east and nearing or crossing the \nAtlantic coast by Monday. This relatively speedy crossing over\nor near our region will most likely keep rainfall amounts from\nbecoming excessive. There does seem to be a lean towards a\nfaster arrival time of the rain, with onset possible around or\nafter midnight Saturday night.\n\nThe 13Z NBM has 48-hour mean rainfall totals through 8 AM \nMonday of between 0.8 and 1.2 inches, with a north-to-south\ngradient. 50th percentile levels are relatively similar to the\nmean values, suggesting less of a bimodal distribution in \nsolutions than from the NBM runs yesterday. However, there has\nbeen an increase in the mean values as compared to yesterday.\n90th percentile levels now fall in the range of 1.75 to 2.25\ninches, also higher than yesterday. This is still below current\n6-hour flash flood guidance values, which are above 2.5 inches\nareawide save for very small pockets. Overall, the threat of\nwidespread flooding concerns remains low, although isolated \nurban and poor drainage issues could in theory arise. In \nparticular, localized street flooding from debris-blocked storm \ndrains could be a problem in the higher-end rainfall scenarios. \nTrends will be monitored. If slower motion of the remnant low \nbecomes more likely, then higher rainfall totals could be more \nof a concern. Severe thunderstorms risks are also very low. The \nprobability of 250 J/kg of SBCAPE remains under 30%, and longer-\nrange machine learning guidance keeps severe probabilities less\nthan 5%.\n\nWith the faster overall motion, any heavy rainfall threat\ncurrently decreases after 12Z Monday as the remnants head to our\neast. Still, relatively light showers may linger into Tuesday as\nsome long-range scenarios bring another shortwave across the\nGreat Lakes.",
